# Directives

**Fail fast and loud.** Never swallow errors or mask failures. Propagate errors to the caller. If something is wrong, crash or log visibly at the point of failure — don't silently produce wrong results.

**Single source of truth.** Never fabricate, fallback, or hardcode a value when a canonical source exists. No fallback chains, no narrower type copies, no hardcoded allowlists or ports. If the authoritative field is empty, leave it empty — don't guess.

**Join on ids, never on names.** When one record points at another, reference it by the id assigned by the database that owns it. Names collide — two skills, two tools, two repos can share one — and a name-based join then picks the wrong row silently, staying correct right up until the second row appears. So before writing a join, find the owning source-of-truth service and take its id from there. If the target has no id, do not invent a local one and do not fall back to the name: first establish that the record should not exist at all, and if it should, create it in the owning store and use the id it hands back. Store the id. Carry the name alongside it only for display, and never read the name back to find the row.

**Layers are transparent.** Adapters, bridges, and transport layers pass data through unchanged. No formatting, truncation, or lossy transforms in intermediate layers. Presentation belongs at the edge.

**Names must tell the truth — rename up the stack.** A name that lies about what code does is a defect, not cosmetics. Whenever a change materially alters what a function/method/module *does* or *can do* (its responsibility, contract, or capability — not an internal tweak), renaming is part of that change, not a follow-up PR. After the change, walk up the call chain — direct callers, wrappers, interface methods, route/CLI handlers, params, docstrings — and update every name the old one no longer describes accurately. Propagate: site → callers → public surface → docs. Scope the rename to what actually changed meaning; don't churn unrelated names. Never defer it as "a rename later" — that PR never comes and the lie ships.

**Completeness over brevity in names.** Prefer the complete, descriptive name over the short one. Do not truncate or abbreviate to save characters: brevity pressure is largely a human ergonomic concern — visible screen width, typing speed, working-memory load — and those concerns barely apply to model-maintained code, so they should not drive naming here. The empirical literature on human readers already favors full words: full-word identifiers measurably speed defect-finding over abbreviations and single letters, and abbreviating buys back none of the comprehension lost by dropping the full word. The one real limit found in that research — that *excessively* long names overload short-term memory — is itself a human-memory effect, so it constrains us even less. Optimize for a name that fully states the concept, not the shortest token that hints at it. This is not license to pad: every word must carry meaning (the truth-telling rule above still binds), but when choosing between a complete name and a terse one, choose complete.

**Surface ambiguity before implementing.** When a task is ambiguous, vague, or admits multiple plausible interpretations, present the options and your assumptions before coding — never silently pick one and start implementing. If two readings of the request lead to materially different work, name them in 1-2 sentences each and ask which. Skip only for trivial edits (single-line fixes, typos, status checks).

**No flattery, no reflexive concession.** Don't open with "Great question", "You're right", "Good catch", or similar warm-ups. When the user pushes back, don't capitulate by default — verify the claim first. If they're right, say so and fix it; if they're wrong or only partly right, say that plainly with the evidence. Agreement without grounding is noise and erodes trust.

**Don't revert another agent's in-progress work.** When you find unfamiliar uncommitted edits, new files, or lines you don't recognize in a file you're touching, treat them as another agent's parallel work — keep them. They may be unrelated changes I want anyway. If your edit conflicts with what's already there, ask before overwriting; never `git checkout`/`git restore`/`git stash drop` someone else's working tree, and never delete files you didn't create. Only touch what your specific task requires.

**Check skill-store before non-trivial tasks.** For any task beyond a trivial edit or question, query the skill-store registry for relevant SKILL.md entries before starting: `curl -s "http://localhost:8301/skills?q=KEYWORDS&limit=10"` (or via dash: `/api/skill-store/skills?q=...`). Pull promising hits with `GET /skills/{id}/files/SKILL.md` and follow their guidance. Skip only for truly trivial work (single-line fixes, status checks, simple questions).

