## Google Calendar

To create, update, or delete Google Calendar events, always use the dash-server API. Never hit the Google Calendar API directly, and **do not use the "claude.ai Google Calendar" MCP connector (or any other MCP calendar tool)** even when one is offered — this endpoint is the only supported path.

**Endpoint:** `POST http://localhost:8101/api/calendar/google`

**Auth:** send `Authorization: Bearer $DASH_TOKEN` (the token dash-server was started with — read it from the running unit's `DASH_TOKEN` env). A `dash_token` or `token` cookie also works. There is no localhost bypass; an unauthenticated call returns 401.

The backend automatically injects the user's timezone from Google Calendar settings if not provided. You do not need to specify a timezone — just use bare ISO datetimes.

```bash
# Create an event
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8101/api/calendar/google \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $DASH_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "summary": "Event title",
    "start": {"dateTime": "2026-04-15T18:30:00"},
    "end": {"dateTime": "2026-04-15T19:00:00"},
    "location": "Optional location",
    "description": "Optional description"
  }'

# Update an event
curl -s -X PUT http://localhost:8101/api/calendar/google/<eventId> \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $DASH_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{ "summary": "Updated title", "start": {"dateTime": "..."}, "end": {"dateTime": "..."} }'

# Delete an event
curl -s -X DELETE http://localhost:8101/api/calendar/google/<eventId> \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $DASH_TOKEN"
```

Auth tokens and timezone are handled by the backend. dash-server resolves the Google OAuth token from **auth-store** (`GET http://127.0.0.1:8303/api/resolve/google?account=default`) and proxies to the Google Calendar API — verified in `dash/server/api_calendar.go`. (This used to go through `apiauth`, which auth-store has since replaced.)

## Scheduler (cron jobs)

Centralized cron scheduler on `localhost:8092`. Auth via `Authorization: Bearer $SCHEDULER_TOKEN` only if the server was started with `SCHEDULER_TOKEN` set (currently unset on this host — omit header). Jobs are stored in SQLite at `~/.scheduler/scheduler.db`.

Two job types: **shell** (executes `sh -c <command>`) and **agent** (invokes a Claude-based agent with a prompt). Schedules use standard 5-field cron (robfig/cron).

A shell job's wall-clock cap is **per-job**: `timeout_seconds` on create or PATCH, `0` meaning the 5-minute default. Set it generously for anything that legitimately runs long (`harness-watch` is 1800; four of the five nightly repo guards are 3600, and `repo-elf-guard` is 600) — the cap is enforced by killing the job's whole process group, so a job that outlives it is killed, not left to run. A tick that arrives while the previous run of that same job is **still in flight is dropped, not stacked**, so a slow job can no longer pile up concurrent copies of itself.

```bash
# List jobs
curl -s http://localhost:8092/api/jobs

# Create a shell job (e.g. poke an HTTP endpoint every 5 min)
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8092/api/jobs \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "name": "disk-root health check",
    "description": "Hits healthcheck /api/check/disk-root",
    "schedule": "*/5 * * * *",
    "type": "shell",
    "command": "curl -sfS -X POST http://localhost:8099/api/check/disk-root"
  }'

# Create an agent job
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8092/api/jobs \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "name": "daily summary",
    "schedule": "0 8 * * *",
    "type": "agent",
    "agent": "claude-code",
    "prompt": "Summarize yesterday's activity…"
  }'

# Run a job immediately
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8092/api/jobs/<id>/run

# Enable/disable
curl -s -X PATCH http://localhost:8092/api/jobs/<id> \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"enabled":false}'

# Give a long-running job its own wall-clock cap (0 = the 5-minute default)
curl -s -X PATCH http://localhost:8092/api/jobs/<id> \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"timeout_seconds":3600}'

# Edit any other field — prompt, schedule, command, agent, model, workspace_id …
curl -s -X PATCH http://localhost:8092/api/jobs/<id> \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"prompt":"…","schedule":"0 6 * * *"}'

# Hold (pause) a job until a future time
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8092/api/jobs/<id>/hold \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"days":7}'     # or {"until":"2026-05-01T00:00:00Z"}
curl -s -X DELETE http://localhost:8092/api/jobs/<id>/hold  # clear hold

# Run history (status, output, timing per run)
curl -s http://localhost:8092/api/jobs/<id>/runs

# Delete job
curl -s -X DELETE http://localhost:8092/api/jobs/<id>
```

`PATCH` takes **every** job field, not just `enabled`. Send only the fields you are changing. It refuses rather than half-obeys: a misspelled field name, an unparseable cron expression, or an edit that would leave the job unrunnable (a shell job with no command, an agent job with no prompt) is a 400. A successful edit reloads the schedule, so it takes effect on the **next tick** — editing the DB by hand and forcing a reload with a no-op PATCH is no longer needed, and the row you edit by hand is now also the row the next run executes.

**Re-measured live 2026-08-13 on a disposable job, and this is now true of the running service too.** For five days this section carried a ⛔ block saying the opposite — that the deployed binary silently dropped every field but three and answered `200` — on the strength of a 2026-08-08 measurement plus the binary's build date. `~/bin/scheduler` was rebuilt **2026-08-11 20:33**, after commit `863868a` landed, and nobody re-measured. Verified this time by creating a throwaway job and reading it back: `PATCH {"schedule":"0 6 * * *","command":"echo CHANGED"}` → the row really changed; `{"scheduel":…}` → **400** `unknown field`; `{"schedule":"not a cron"}` → **400**; `{"command":""}` on a shell job → **400** `command is required for shell jobs`. The DB-surgery workaround is obsolete, as originally written. **A build date is not a deploy check — re-measure.**

⚠️ **You cannot create a disabled job through the API.** The `POST /api/jobs` request struct has no `enabled` field at all (`internal/api/api.go`) and `db.go` sets `Enabled = true` on insert. The strict decoder rejects `{"enabled":false}` with a **400** `json: unknown field "enabled"` (measured live 2026-08-13), so you learn it failed rather than getting a live cron job you believed was off. Create it, then PATCH `{"enabled":false}`.

Shell jobs return status `success` or `error` based on exit code; `output` field in run history captures combined stdout+stderr (use `-f` with curl so 5xx becomes non-zero exit → `error` status → visible in `/runs`). Two further statuses mean the run did not finish on its own terms: `timeout` (it exceeded `timeout_seconds` and its process group was killed) and `interrupted` (the scheduler process died mid-run — a crash, an OOM kill, a deploy restart — and the run was closed out at the next startup). A run is never left in `running` by a process that is gone.

## Noteboard (notes, todos, ranked lists)

Canonical store for personal notes, todos, and ranked lists. Agents should read and write directly to the noteboard REST API on `localhost:8191`. Dash also proxies it at `/api/noteboard/*` for the browser UI, and *that* surface is behind dash's auth. ⚠️ noteboard itself has no auth at all, and it is not on the local socket — it listens on `*:8191` with no firewall rule, so anything that can route to this host has full read and write.

Every item has a `type` of `note`, `todo`, `rank`, or `workspace`. Title is required; body is markdown. Status defaults to `open`; use `done` to complete a todo and `archived` to archive one.

**Deletion in this store does not destroy data.** `DELETE` is reversible: it stamps `deleted_at` and the row stays, dropping out of every read path (list, get, search, tags, lists, count). It does *not* touch `status` — archiving is a state you chose for a live item, deletion is the item being taken away, and collapsing the two means a restore can't tell them apart. `POST /api/items/{id}/restore` brings it back in exactly the state it was deleted in. Every update, delete, restore, hold, unhold and purge writes a snapshot to `item_revisions`, readable at `GET /api/items/{id}/revisions`. `?hard=true` still purges the row, but snapshots first — reach for it only for genuinely heavy data.

⚠️ **An UPDATE can still destroy data, so read before you overwrite.** The snapshot is partial: it covers 8 of the 20 item columns (`title`, `body`, `tags`, `status`, `priority`, `list_id`, `parent_id`, `links`) and **not** `rank`, `due_at` or `schedule`. Overwrite an item's `rrule` and the old rule is gone from the database entirely — no revision holds it. `POST /api/items/rerank` writes `rank` with no snapshot at all.

⚠️ **`GET /api/items` and `/api/search` hide held items by default.** Nothing in a default listing tells you so. `include_held=true` reveals them, and held-ness is inherited down `parent_id`. That default is deliberate — a hold is the user parking work behind a gate — so an unattended worker should leave it alone rather than widen its own search with it.

A `workspace` is an agent's durable working memory: a timestamped markdown document a recurring job reads first and rewrites last on every run. It's a distinct type so it can never be mistaken for work to do (it must not pollute the todo queue) nor clutter the notes list. Scheduler agent jobs point at one via `workspace_id`, and the executor injects its location and contract into the prompt automatically. Use `parent_id` to split an overgrown workspace into children with a roll-up left behind, so the memory always stays small enough for a human to read in one sitting.

```bash
# List items (optionally filter by type/tag/status/list_id)
curl -s "http://localhost:8191/api/items?type=todo&status=open&limit=100"

# Create a todo
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8191/api/items \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"type":"todo","title":"Call plumber","tags":["home"],"priority":2}'

# Create a note with markdown body
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8191/api/items \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"type":"note","title":"Deploy playbook","body":"## Steps\n1. …","tags":["ops"]}'

# Mark a todo done
curl -s -X PATCH http://localhost:8191/api/items/<id> \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"status":"done"}'

# Delete (reversible — stamps deleted_at, leaves status alone)
curl -s -X DELETE http://localhost:8191/api/items/<id>

# Undo the delete; comes back exactly as it was
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8191/api/items/<id>/restore

# Every prior state of an item (update / delete / restore)
curl -s http://localhost:8191/api/items/<id>/revisions

# Full-text search
curl -s "http://localhost:8191/api/search?q=plumber&type=todo"

# All unique tags / named lists
curl -s http://localhost:8191/api/tags
curl -s http://localhost:8191/api/lists

# Reorder items in a ranked list
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8191/api/items/rerank \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"items":[{"id":"<id1>","rank":1.0},{"id":"<id2>","rank":2.0}]}'
```

DB: `~/.noteboard/noteboard.db` (SQLite + FTS5). Single source of truth — do NOT reintroduce a filesystem-based notes tree.

### Recurrence (`schedule`) — RFC 5545

Any item can carry a `schedule`: the rule for when it is due and when it nudges. It is available on every item type, not just personal ones — `tag` / `exclude_tag` is what separates personal from coding todos, not the presence of a rule.

`due_at` stays the single source of truth for **when an item is next due**. `schedule` is the rule that *generates* that answer; `dtstart` is the series **anchor** (iCalendar sense), never the current due date.

```bash
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8191/api/items \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"type":"todo","title":"Water the plants","tags":["personal","home"],
       "schedule":{
         "dtstart":"2026-07-21T09:00:00-07:00",
         "tzid":"America/Los_Angeles",
         "rrule":"FREQ=MONTHLY;BYDAY=3TU",
         "mode":"instance",
         "remind":{"lead":["-P1D"],"channels":["digest","calendar"]}
       }}'

# Preview when a rule ACTUALLY fires — do this before trusting one.
curl -s "http://localhost:8191/api/items/<id>/occurrences?from=2026-07-01T00:00:00-07:00&to=2027-01-01T00:00:00-08:00"
```

- **`rrule`** — any RFC 5545 rule. `FREQ=MONTHLY;BYDAY=3TU` (every 3rd Tuesday), `FREQ=WEEKLY;INTERVAL=2;BYDAY=TH` (every other Thursday), `FREQ=MONTHLY;BYDAY=MO,TU,WE,TH,FR;BYSETPOS=-1` (last weekday of the month). End with `;COUNT=n` or `;UNTIL=...`. Use `rdate` for specific one-off dates and `exdate` to skip an occurrence.
- **`tzid`** — **mandatory** whenever a rule is present, and never defaulted. An offset is not a zone: a rule anchored to `-07:00` cannot know what happens after a DST transition and silently drifts an hour twice a year.
- **`mode`** — `instance` (default): each occurrence is materialized as a **child todo**, so downstream consumers just see ordinary todos and never need to understand recurrence. `rolling`: one row whose `due_at` advances when completed (no history).
- **`remind.nag`** — an RRULE that repeats **until the item is done** ("bother me every weekday at 10am"). Independent of the due date in both its rule and its expansion: `rdate` and `exdate` apply to the **due series only**. Fixed 2026-08-13 — `Schedule.expand` used to read those two sets off the schedule and apply them to whichever rule it expanded, nag included, so an item due 2026-08-10 with `nag FREQ=DAILY;COUNT=2` and `rdate ["2026-12-25T09:00-08:00"]` nagged three times, the third straight off the due schedule. They are now parameters passed per series and `NagOccurrences` passes neither, pinned by `TestDueDateOverridesDoNotMoveTheNag`. ⚠️ `reminder-coordinator` links noteboard's `model` package through a `replace` directive, so **the live reminders only change when that binary is rebuilt** — check it was redeployed before trusting this paragraph.
- **`remind.lead`** — ISO-8601 offsets before due (`-P1D`), calendar-aware.
- **`remind.channels`** — defaults to `["digest"]`. `calendar` and `herald` are opt-in per item.

A bad rule is rejected at write time — it would otherwise expand to nothing and silently remind no one. Always check `/occurrences` before trusting a rule; `BYDAY=3TU` and `BYDAY=TU;BYSETPOS=3` read alike and diverge in months that start on a Tuesday.

## Reminders — one coordinator, never a new cron job

`~/bin/reminder-coordinator` (scheduler job 32, `*/15`) is the **only** thing that reminds the user of anything. It materializes recurring todos, rewrites the standing **"📆 Today — personal"** note (the primary surface — always current, readable at dash `/notes`), mirrors opted-in items to Google Calendar, and sends **at most one herald session per tick**.

It replaced five jobs that each nudged without knowing what the others had said, so an overdue todo with its own nudge job opened two chat sessions a day.

**Do not add a cron job to nudge about a todo.** That is what recreated the bug. Put the cadence on the todo as `schedule.remind.nag` and the coordinator will deliver it in its single session. Source: `~/repos/scheduler/cmd/reminder-coordinator/`.

